sels cleared for foreign ports, which means I'll need to focus on the coastal and overseas port data.\n\nThe key insight is that the grand total row represents total tonnage cleared from the US for all destinations, including Canadian and foreign ports. The \"cleared to coastal and overseas ports\" row would represent foreign ports specifically.\n\nI'll calculate the percentage by comparing American vessels to the grand total across the three months, confirming the initial calculation shows 26.7% when rounded to one decimal place.\n\nI'm carefully examining the table's structure to verify the \"direct for foreign ports\" interpretation, noting the specific categories of port clearance and vessel classification.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_42fb1df8b7dad812","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"todo__todo_write","arguments":{"content":"- [x] Examine available Treasury Bulletin resources\n- [x] Find data on American vessels tonnage for Jan-Mar 1941\n- [x] Find grand total tonnage for Jan-Mar 1941\n- [x] Calculate percentage of American vessels vs total\n- [x] Compute Pearson correlation coefficient\n- [x] Format and write final answer to answer.txt"}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"todo"}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
